WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.060
<v Instructor>Let's talk about AWS Secrets Manager,</v>

2
00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.830
which is a great service meant, obviously,

3
00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:05.910
for storing secrets.

4
00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.430
It could be passwords, API keys, and so on.

5
00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:09.630
But the cool thing about the service

6
00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.210
is that you can rotate your secrets automatically

7
00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:13.770
every X number of days.

8
00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:18.030
And these new secrets can be rotated on-demand

9
00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.190
or automatically and generated automatically.

10
00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:22.380
For example, using a Lambda function.

11
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:23.490
There is a native integration

12
00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:27.420
between RDS and Secrets Manager for all DB engines,

13
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.970
but also Redshift, DocumentDB and so on.

14
00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.770
And for other databases or services

15
00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.510
that you want to rotate automatically,

16
00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.580
you would have to write a custom Lambda function

17
00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:36.990
that would generate these secrets,

18
00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:39.840
and then insert it into Secrets Manager.

19
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.090
Here, in this diagram, we have Secrets Manager.

20
00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:44.460
It contains a secret, it's a database password.

21
00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.480
And it's actually the database password of our RDS database.

22
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.390
So they're linked together, and they will change together.

23
00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.340
Now, to control access to the secret,

24
00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.040
we can use a resource-based policy.

25
00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.440
And also, Secrets Manager is going to be integrated

26
00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.270
with so many other services

27
00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.700
that will automatically pull the secrets

28
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:03.773
from Secrets Manager.

29
00:01:03.773 --> 00:01:05.070
We have CloudFormation,

30
00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.230
so you can reference Secrets Manager secrets

31
00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:08.550
in the CloudFormation templates,

32
00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.550
but also CodeBuild, ECS for your environment variables.

33
00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.083
And I will not name them all, but EMR,

34
00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:15.870
Fargate, EKS, Parameter Store, and so on.

35
00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:16.770
So in this example,

36
00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.960
I represented an ECS task.

37
00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:19.793
And as you can see,

38
00:01:19.793 --> 00:01:23.220
this ECS task will need to access your RDS database.

39
00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.360
So how will it do it?

40
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.980
Well, because we have a native integration

41
00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.260
between ECS and Secrets Manager,

42
00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.210
what can happen is that at boot time,

43
00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.940
your ECS task can automatically pull your secrets

44
00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.660
and inject it as an environment variable within your task.

45
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:37.493
And therefore,

46
00:01:37.493 --> 00:01:39.210
because it's available as an environment variable,

47
00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:42.660
then your ECS task, whatever software is running within it,

48
00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.003
can access your RDS database securely.

49
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.920
This is the kind of template that would create a secret

50
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.076
with Secrets Manager within CloudFormation.

51
00:01:52.076 --> 00:01:54.633
In the first part, we generate the secrets.

52
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:57.150
Then, in the second part,

53
00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.950
we're going to reference the secrets

54
00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.320
in your RDS DB instance.

55
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:02.730
And then, in the third part,

56
00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.890
we create what's called a "secret attachment",

57
00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.830
which is going to link the secrets to the RDS DB instance.

58
00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.290
And it's going to tell Secrets Manager that,

59
00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:11.490
in case of rotation,

60
00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.500
you need to also change the password

61
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.050
in your RDS database itself.

62
00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.400
You need to know the process

63
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.450
to share secrets across accounts in Secrets Manager.

64
00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.220
So you have a security account,

65
00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.110
where your secret is going to be,

66
00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:28.830
and a developer account that needs to use that secret.

67
00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:31.560
We have a Secret Manager's secret, it's in our account,

68
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:32.393
and of course,

69
00:02:32.393 --> 00:02:36.360
it's protected and encrypted through a KMS key.

70
00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.090
Now, how do we make a user in the dev account

71
00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:41.220
access our secret?

72
00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.020
There is no way to share that secret

73
00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:44.940
using Resource Access Manager,

74
00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:46.470
so you can forget about it.

75
00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.140
Instead, we can access and we can attach

76
00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.173
resource-based policies to our secrets.

77
00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.050
The first thing to do though,

78
00:02:55.050 --> 00:03:00.050
is to allow the user to do a kms:Decrypt on our KMS key

79
00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:04.710
by attaching a KMS policy to our KMS key.

80
00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:05.880
And on top of it,

81
00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.800
you see there will be a condition in it.

82
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.140
The KMS via service,

83
00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:14.550
which allows to use the kms:Decrypt operation

84
00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.700
only if it's invoked via, so through,

85
00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.923
the Service Secrets Manager.

86
00:03:19.923 --> 00:03:23.130
It's a little bit of a tricky condition here.

87
00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:26.340
And then, we create a resource-based policy

88
00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.350
on our Secrets Manager's secrets,

89
00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:31.320
which is similar, for example, to an S3 bucket policy.

90
00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.680
And we allow, from the resource-based policy,

91
00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.500
a user, or an IAM role, or whatever you want,

92
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.150
in the developer account,

93
00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:42.720
to do a GetSecretValue on our secrets.

94
00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.290
And with these two permissions, anyone or, I think,

95
00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:49.890
anyone authorized in a dev account to access our secret

96
00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:51.570
can access it and can decrypt it

97
00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:54.060
because you have access both to the secret,

98
00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.800
thanks to resource-based policy,

99
00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.713
as well as the key, thanks to a KMS policy.

100
00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.190
You can ask me what's the difference

101
00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:04.230
between Parameter Store and Secrets Manager?

102
00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:05.730
Well, Secrets Manager is more expensive,

103
00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.880
but you get automated rotation of secrets with Lambda.

104
00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.310
The Lambda function is automatically provided for you

105
00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.230
with RDS, Redshift, and DocumentDB.

106
00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.000
That's why it's automatic.

107
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.030
Then, of course, you need to encrypt your secrets with KMS.

108
00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.320
It's mandatory.

109
00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.230
And it has deep integration with CloudFormation.

110
00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:25.890
So with the SSM Parameter Store, it's a more simple API.

111
00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.270
There is no secret rotation,

112
00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:28.650
although we'll see in the next slide

113
00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.890
how you can actually create some kind of rotation mechanism

114
00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:34.140
using EventBridge and Lambda.

115
00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:35.490
KMS encryption is optional.

116
00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.910
That means that you can also store non-secret values

117
00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:40.350
in the Parameter Store.

118
00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:41.910
There is deep integration with CloudFormation.

119
00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:43.350
And there is a little trick

120
00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:45.690
to pull Secrets Manager's secret directly

121
00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.253
using the Parameter Store API.

122
00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:50.790
So in terms of rotation, if you look at Secrets Manager,

123
00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.280
there is, for example, deep integration with RDS.

124
00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:55.050
And the way it works that, in the backend,

125
00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:58.260
a Lambda function is going to be invoked every 30 days.

126
00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.020
It's going to change the password onto Amazon RDS

127
00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:04.170
and also change the passwords in Secrets Manager.

128
00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:06.210
Whereas, if you used the SSM Parameter Store...

129
00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:09.720
Say you had your RDS secrets into the Parameter Store.

130
00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:13.320
Well, you would need to create an Amazon EventBridge rule

131
00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.780
to invoke a Lambda function every 30 days

132
00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:18.510
that would change the password and change the value.

133
00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:21.930
But it's up to you to maintain this kind of integration,

134
00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:25.290
whereas it's provided by Secrets Manager automatically

135
00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:26.643
as a rotation feature.

136
00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:28.860
That's it for this lecture.

137
00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:29.940
I hope you liked it,

138
00:05:29.940 --> 00:05:31.890
and I will see you in the next lecture.

